{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:Observations.CrossingEnergyDecomposition","@type":"LeanModule","name":"Observations.CrossingEnergyDecomposition","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.ExpDeriv","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic","lean:module:QOU.Braiding.crossing_energy_channels"],"declarations":19,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.ratio_two_one_ne_tanh","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.ratio_two_one_ne_tanh","kind":"theorem","line":216,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.lean#L216-L224","doc":"The multiplicity factor is genuinely non-trivial: a composite with two\npositive and one negative crossing has ratio `3·tanh(ln q)`, not\n`tanh(ln q)`. Concrete witness that `ratio_general` is not vacuous. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.ratio_general","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.ratio_general","kind":"theorem","line":197,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.lean#L197-L214","doc":"**Scope limit of the ratio identity.** For a composite with mixed\ncrossing signs the ratio picks up the multiplicity factor `(p+m)/(p-m)`:\n\n  `E_asym / E_sym = ((p+m)/(p-m)) · tanh(ln q)`.\n\nThe ratio equals `tanh(ln q)` exactly on the positive-braid locus\n`m = 0` (and, symmetrically, `-tanh(ln q)` on `p = 0`); it is **not**\nindependent of the composite in general. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.ratio_positive_braid","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.ratio_positive_braid","kind":"theorem","line":183,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.lean#L183-L195","doc":"**Ratio identity, correctly scoped.** For a *positive braid closure*\n(`m = 0`, every crossing positive) with at least one crossing, the\nantisymmetric-to-symmetric ratio is `tanh(ln q)`, independent of the\ncrossing number `p`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.asymChannel_mirror","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.asymChannel_mirror","kind":"theorem","line":175,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.lean#L175-L181","doc":"The mirror `σ` fixes the antisymmetric channel — this is the sign-blindness\nof the `sinh` part, and the reason a particle–antiparticle pair retains a\nnet Coulomb energy. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.symChannel_mirror","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.symChannel_mirror","kind":"theorem","line":168,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.lean#L168-L173","doc":"The mirror `σ` (which reverses every crossing, i.e. swaps `p` and `m`)\nnegates the symmetric channel. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.crossingEnergy_split","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.crossingEnergy_split","kind":"theorem","line":161,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.lean#L161-L166","doc":"The composite splits into its two channels. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:crossing-energy-decomposition","label":"prop:crossing-energy-decomposition","md":"quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.antisymmetric_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.antisymmetric_pos","kind":"theorem","line":135,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.lean#L135-L141","doc":"Antisymmetric weight is positive for q > 1. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.symmetric_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.symmetric_pos","kind":"theorem","line":127,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.lean#L127-L133","doc":"Symmetric weight is positive for q > 0. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.em_strong_ratio_tanh","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.em_strong_ratio_tanh","kind":"theorem","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.lean#L116-L125","doc":"EM/strong ratio equals tanh(ln q) for q > 1.\nProof: tanh(ln q) = (e^{ln q} - e^{-ln q})/(e^{ln q} + e^{-ln q})\n       = (q - q⁻¹)/(q + q⁻¹) = emStrongRatio q. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.emStrongRatio","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.emStrongRatio","kind":"def","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.lean#L112-L114","doc":"The EM/strong ratio is (q - q⁻¹)/(q + q⁻¹). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.qbracket_eq_sinh_ratio","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.qbracket_eq_sinh_ratio","kind":"theorem","line":99,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.lean#L99-L110","doc":"The q-bracket as a ratio of hyperbolic sines:\n[n]_q = (qⁿ − q⁻ⁿ)/(q − q⁻¹) = sinh(n·ln q)/sinh(ln q), for q > 0.\nThis is the precise sense in which the algebraic quantum integer is\narchimedean (hyperbolic-geometric) — the bridge underlying the\nKashaev volume realisation (`kashaev-as-archimedean-realization`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.two_sinh_nat_log","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.two_sinh_nat_log","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.lean#L90-L97","doc":"The quantum-integer numerator qⁿ − q⁻ⁿ = 2·sinh(n·ln q), for q > 0.\nThe algebraic Hecke datum is twice a hyperbolic sine. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.q_pow_eq_cosh_add_sinh","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.q_pow_eq_cosh_add_sinh","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.lean#L80-L88","doc":"Algebraic→hyperbolic bridge (powers): for q > 0 and n : ℕ,\nqⁿ = cosh(n·ln q) + sinh(n·ln q). The algebraic q-power is the\nexponential of a hyperbolic length n·ln q. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.q_inv_eq_cosh_sub_sinh","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.q_inv_eq_cosh_sub_sinh","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.lean#L72-L78","doc":"General identity: q⁻¹ = cosh(ln q) - sinh(ln q), for any q > 0. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.q_eq_cosh_add_sinh","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.q_eq_cosh_add_sinh","kind":"theorem","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.lean#L62-L70","doc":"General identity: q = cosh(ln q) + sinh(ln q), for any q > 0.\nThis is `eˣ = cosh x + sinh x` at x = ln q (so the RHS is e^{ln q} = q).\nIt holds for every q > 0 — it is NOT specific to the substrate value. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.antisymmetricWeight_eq_sinh","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.antisymmetricWeight_eq_sinh","kind":"theorem","line":52,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.lean#L52-L60","doc":"The antisymmetric weight (q - q⁻¹)/2 equals sinh(ln q), for q > 0.\nThe \"Coulomb / sinh channel\" is therefore a derived identity — the\nantisymmetric crossing weight IS the hyperbolic sine of λ = ln q,\nnot a fitted parameter. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.symmetricWeight_eq_cosh","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.symmetricWeight_eq_cosh","kind":"theorem","line":42,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.lean#L42-L50","doc":"The symmetric weight (q + q⁻¹)/2 equals cosh(ln q), for q > 0.\nThis is what makes the \"cosh channel\" name a derived identity, not a\ndefinition: the symmetric crossing weight IS the hyperbolic cosine of\nthe crossing rapidity λ = ln q. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.q_inv_decomposition","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.q_inv_decomposition","kind":"theorem","line":35,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.lean#L35-L40","doc":"q⁻¹ decomposes as cosh - sinh. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.q_decomposition","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.q_decomposition","kind":"theorem","line":28,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/crossing-energy-decomposition.lean#L28-L33","doc":"q decomposes as cosh + sinh. ","signatureChunk":0}]}